-- title:
--   ̄ j(q^{Nℓ}) lies in K(̄ j(q),̄ j(q^N))
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field whose characteristic is a prime $\ell$, and let $N$ be a nonzero natural number. Inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K = K((q))$ of Hahn series over $K$ with integer exponents, write `jqModC K` for the series $q^{-1}$ times the image of the integral power series `jNum` under the ring map $\mathbb{Z} \to K$, i.e. the reduction $\bar j(q)$ of the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant, and for $M \neq 0$ write `jqNModC K M` for its image $\bar j(q^M)$ under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K M`, which rescales exponents by multiplication by $M$ (the substitution $q \mapsto q^M$). Let `modularFunctionFieldC K N` be the intermediate field of $K((q))$ over $K$ generated by the two elements $\bar j(q)$ and $\bar j(q^N)$. The theorem asserts that $\bar j(q^{N\ell})$, that is `jqNModC K (N * ℓ)`, belongs to this intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldC K N`. Only membership is asserted; no statement is made about equality of the fields generated at level $N\ell$ and level $N$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion form, in characteristic $\ell$, of the fact that one component of the reduction of $X_0(N\ell) \to X_0(N)$ is the Frobenius: passing from level $N$ to level $N\ell$ adjoins nothing new, because $\bar j(q^{N\ell})$ is the $\ell$-th power of $\bar j(q^N)$. It is used in the construction of prolongation tuples for place specialisations on modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_jqNModC_mul_mem.lean

